_test -- experimental configurations
====================================

Everything here derives from one of the three finished configs in src/
(OpenSPARC_T1_rebuild, t1_boot_fix, tlb_fix) and has not been promoted out
of _test.  Each subdirectory has its own README saying what it changes and
why.  This file says what the directory as a whole is worth, which is a
different question -- and the short answer is: less than the per-config
READMEs alone might suggest.

Read this before running anything here.


Two families, and they do not run on the same machine
-----------------------------------------------------

  old hv MD format          maxcpu_fix, pagesize_256m, post_t1boot_mini,
  (no content-version)      post_tlb_fix, post_tlb_fix2

      A single-level hv config: one guest node carrying cpuset, membase,
      memsize, realbase, uartbase and pdpa inline, emitted by the GUEST()
      macro in common.hdesc.  This is Sun's 2006 shape and it runs on the
      firmware in fw_blobs/ -- the hypervisor Sun shipped with the
      OpenSPARC T1 release, built for the Legion simulator.  These are the
      configs the boot matrix in docs/index.html was measured on.

  new hv MD format          hvmd_v1, hvmd_v1_pciex
  (content-version 1.0)

      Two-level.  An hv MD describing guests, cpus, memory, devices,
      ldc_endpoints and consoles as container nodes, plus a separate guest
      MD that the hypervisor finds through the guest's mdpa property.  The
      hv MD's content-version is a u64 (0x100000000); the guest MD's is the
      string "1.0" -- different types, same idea.

      Nothing in fw_blobs/ can run these.  See the next section.


The new-format configs need a completely different set of binaries
------------------------------------------------------------------

They are not drop-in replacements for the old ones, and none of what they
need is in this repository:

  q.bin       Our own build of the OpenSPARC *T2* release hypervisor
              (hypervisor/src/greatlakes/ontario), with stock GNU cross
              tools and a non-default option set.  Sun's shipped q.bin is
              the T1 release's Legion build and rejects these configs
              outright --

                  ABORT: config-basics: MD content-version not found

              -- because that format predates the version field entirely.
              The reverse holds too: the T2-generation hypervisor rejects
              the old-format configs for the same reason.

  reset.bin   Likewise rebuilt from the T2 sources.

  openboot.bin
              Sun's, but from the T2 release, not the OBP 4.20.0 in
              fw_blobs/.  Two are usable and the choice matters:

                obp/obp/arch/sun4v/niagara/t1_fpga/openboot.bin
                    the 2009 Ontario build; carries sun4v-vdisk,
                    sun4v-vnet, sun4v-chan-dev.  What hvmd_v1 booted on.

                obp/obp/arch/sun4v/niagara2/release/openboot.bin
                    additionally carries the pciex word, which loads
                    sun4v-vpci.  Required by hvmd_v1_pciex: without it OBP
                    does not walk a PCIe bus at all.  Runs on the T1 CPU
                    model and boots Solaris just as well; its N2-specific
                    words stay inert because no MD node matches them.

  qemu        Upstream, plus the sun4v TTE page-size fix, plus a patch
              modelling the hypervisor's own private console UART at
              0xfff0c2c000 -- a compile-time constant in the T2 sources,
              nothing to do with any MD.  Without that device the hv hangs
              on its first PRINT() and the machine is silent.

So this is a transplant: a hypervisor generation from the T2 release, built
for and running on the T1 machine QEMU emulates.  That is what makes the LDC
and PCIe work possible at all -- the T1-release hypervisor has no LDC code
in it -- and it is equally why these configs cannot be tried by pointing
QEMU at this repository alone.  The hypervisor build recipe is kept
elsewhere and is not published.


What has and has not been seen to boot
--------------------------------------

The old-format configs are the measured ones; results are in the boot matrix
and the artefact logs it cites.

For the new-format pair, precision matters, because "it booted" is easy to
inherit across an edit that was never booted:

  hvmd_v1         The blob pair that booted Solaris 10 to a login prompt --
                  first the 2005 disk.s10hw2 image, then the T2 release's
                  Solaris 10 8/07 disk1.img with the whole LDoms driver
                  stack -- is one property away from what is committed here.
                  The guest MD is byte-identical to the one that booted; the
                  hv MD differs by perfctraccess alone, changed 1 -> 0 after
                  those boots (see below).  The change withdraws a permission
                  rather than granting one, so it cannot plausibly break
                  anything -- but it has not been re-run.

  hvmd_v1_pciex   Has never booted Solaris.  Its recorded result stops at the
                  OBP ok prompt: OBP builds /pci@0 from the cfg-handle, loads
                  sun4v-vpci, issues VPCI_CONFIG_GET, and the hypervisor
                  answers EINVAL.  That negative result is what the config
                  exists to produce.

The work since 2026-08-04 has gone into getting hardware described and
enumerated -- LDC endpoints, a PCIe root complex, interrupt maps -- rather
than into re-testing the boot after each step.  Read everything under
bin/_test/ as "builds, and is what the source says", not as "known to boot".
Which of it survives contact with a running Solaris is the next question,
not an answered one.


perfctraccess: the old configs keep 1 on purpose
------------------------------------------------

hvmd_v1 and hvmd_v1_pciex set perfctraccess = 0 on the guest node; every
old-format config still sets 1, from the GUEST() macro.  Neither is an
oversight.

Non-zero lets hcall_niagara_getperf / setperf past their
GUEST_PERFREG_ACCESSIBLE check, after which the hypervisor -- both
generations; T1 release in greatlakes/common/src/hcall.s, T2 release in
ontario/src/hcall_niagara.s -- performs a raw *physical* access to real T1
registers: JBI_PERF_CTL at 0x8000020000, DRAM_PERF_CTL0..3 at 0x9700000400
and up.  QEMU's -M niagara maps neither address, and an unassigned access
there is not a silent zero: it raises a data access exception with HS_PRIV
set, i.e. inside the hypervisor.  A guest reaches it by ordinary means --
Solaris creates dram/jbus kstats once the Niagara CPU API group registers,
so `kstat -p dram:::` is enough.

Sun's own configs disagree with each other, and both are right: the T1 FPGA
descriptors ship 1, because that board has a real JBus interface and a real
DRAM controller behind those addresses; the Legion config ships 0, because
the simulator has neither.  QEMU is the Legion case, so 0 is the correct
value for anything written here.

The old-format configs are nevertheless left at 1.  OpenSPARC_T1_rebuild is
Sun's 2006 source copied verbatim and has to stay that way -- the
byte-identity proof against bin/_reference/ is what it exists for -- and
every other old config descends from it through the patch chains under
patches/.  Changing the property there would mean regenerating thirteen
patch sets to close a hazard that nothing in the boot matrix has ever
triggered, and it would cost the one guarantee the tree is built around.
Documented instead.
